# ime-1.103.101.0-to-1.104.102.0

## Scope

This document describes the changes found between these Intune Management Extension packages:

| Package | Version |
|---|---:|
| Previous package | `1.103.101.0` |
| New package | `1.104.102.0` |

Methodology is the same as the prior two reports in this series: MSI table comparison (`msiinfo`/`msidump`), extracted CAB payloads, ECMA-335 metadata table row counts for managed assemblies, string-heap diffing, and — new for this release — direct inspection of the shipped `.mof` (WMI schema) and `.json` (manifest) files, which are plain text and required no reverse engineering at all. **A second pass** (see the addendum after section 1) added a fourth source: the actual `IntuneWindowsAgentCustomActions` binary stream extracted from both MSIs, which gives us the real managed assembly (`SideCarSetupCustomActions.dll`) behind every custom action, rather than just its name and MSI-table wiring. No decompiler was available, so IL-level logic changes still cannot be shown directly; findings remain structural (new types/methods/fields, new strings, new files, new WMI class definitions, new config entries) but are now cross-checked against two independently-produced extractions of the same packages.

This is an independent reverse-engineering report. Presence of code does not mean a feature is active for every tenant.

## Executive summary

This is the largest release in the series so far — package size grew from 8.0 MB to 13.1 MB, and **17 new files were added** (nothing was removed). Three changes stand out:

1. **IME now ships the client-side plumbing for Quick Machine Recovery (QMR)**, part of Microsoft's Windows Resiliency Initiative. A new WMI/DSC provider called `IntunePolicyAdapter` is registered under a new WMI namespace, a `Microsoft.Intune.WinRe.Bundle.cab` (3.85 MB) containing WinRE-side recovery components is staged locally, and a Rust-compiled native helper (`dynpin_cxx.dll`) validates the payload signer and TLS pinning before that bundle would ever be trusted. This matches Microsoft's publicly documented QMR feature (Windows 11 24H2+, configured via the `RemoteRemediation` CSP through Intune).
2. **A new "ComponentManager" and "Policy" runtime is now hosted inside the main agent process.** `Microsoft.Management.Services.IntuneWindowsAgent.exe` gained a `ComponentManagerLoader`/`ComponentRuntimeExecutionManagerFactory` and an `IIntunePolicyBackgroundExecutionManager`, each with its own dedicated log file (`ComponentManager.log`, `Operations.log`) wired into the shipped `.exe.config`.
3. **Win32 app enforcement gets a new distributed-tracing-style "OperationSpan" model** plus explicit download-timing telemetry keys, and the main agent adds a batched telemetry layer for IC3/Trouter connection, logger-error, and message-processing events.

