## 1. Quick Machine Recovery / Windows Resiliency Initiative plumbing

This is the headline change, and it's directly readable — the `.mof` and `.json` files shipped in the MSI are plain text.

### A new WMI/DSC provider: `IntunePolicyAdapter`

`IntunePolicyAdapter.mof` registers a Win32 WMI provider (`ClsId {6B5A70A8-98F6-4D59-9A02-179AFE94A58E}`, `HostingModel = "LocalServiceHost"`) inside a brand-new namespace, created by `IntunePolicyAdapterNs.mof`:

```text
#pragma namespace("\\\\.\\root")
[NamespaceSecuritySDDL("O:BAG:BAD:P(A;;0x6001F;;;SY)")]
instance of __Namespace
{
    Name = "MicrosoftDeviceManagement_IntuneExtensibility";
};
```

The provider implements a DSC-style (`OMI_BaseResource`-derived) resource class, verbatim from the shipped MOF:

```text
class IntunePolicyAdapter : OMI_BaseResource
{
    string MeID;                 // "Required configuration document MMP-C MeID."
    string DocumentID;            // "Required configuration document ID."
    string Version;               // "Configuration document Version."
    string PayloadName = "IntunePayload";
    string SettingReportIDs = ""; // split on ';' for multi-ID reporting
    string ExtendedProperties = "{}";
    string IntunePayload;         // "actions to be taken on underlying WMI providers"
    string UserSid = "";
    string Ensure = "Present";    // Present | Absent

    uint32 GetTargetResource(...);
    uint32 TestTargetResource(...);
    uint32 SetTargetResource(...);
};
```

This is the same Get/Test/Set resource pattern used by Windows PowerShell DSC and by the MDM CSP-to-WMI bridge. In plain terms: Intune can now push an arbitrary "configuration document" (`IntunePayload`) to this local WMI class, and the provider is responsible for applying it to "underlying WMI providers" and reporting state back. `UninstallIntunePolicyAdapter.mof` cleanly removes the namespace on uninstall (`#pragma deleteinstance`).

No MSI custom action explicitly *registers* the provider — only `UnregisterIntunePolicyAdapter` exists in `CustomAction`/`InstallExecuteSequence`. **Confirmed in the addendum below**: the custom-action assembly itself shows the signature of spawning `mofcomp.exe` as a child process (with a timeout) specifically inside the *uninstall* path, and `#pragma autorecover` in `IntunePolicyAdapter.mof` is the standard Windows mechanism that lets WMI's CIMOM recompile a class automatically the first time it's needed — so install-time registration doesn't require its own explicit action, but a clean uninstall does. `IntunePolicyAdapter.dll` also exports the standard COM self-registration entry points `DllRegisterServer`/`DllUnregisterServer`/`Adapter_RegisterDLL`/`Adapter_UnRegisterDLL` plus the WMI provider entry point `MI_Main`, consistent with this picture.

### A native policy storage backend with its own signature verification

`IntunePolicyStorage.dll` is native code (not managed — it exports `GetPolicyPayload`, `GetAllPoliciesPayload`, `GetAllPoliciesSettingsAggregatedPayload`, and, notably, `VerifyFileIsMicrosoftSigned`). The binary also contains roughly a dozen embedded SHA-256-shaped hex strings sitting immediately after that verification function in the string table — consistent with a pinned allow-list of trusted certificate/file hashes, though their exact purpose cannot be confirmed without disassembly.

### A staged Windows Recovery Environment (WinRE) bundle

`Microsoft.Intune.WinRe.Bundle.json` is a readable manifest describing the contents of the co-located `Microsoft.Intune.WinRe.Bundle.cab`:

```text
Component: Microsoft.Intune.WinRe.Bundle, Version 1.0.0
Files: Microsoft.Intune.WinRe.Component.dll, Microsoft.Intune.WinRe.Core.dll,
       Microsoft.Intune.WinRe.Framework.dll, Microsoft.Intune.WinRe.Registration.dll,
       Microsoft.Management.Clients.Common.Base.dll,
       Microsoft.Management.Clients.Policy.OfflineReader.dll,
       IntuneRemoteManagementPlugin\{Amd64,Arm64,I386}\MicrosoftIntuneWinReRemoteManagementPlugin.dll,
       IntuneRemoteManagementPlugin\{Amd64,Arm64,I386}\CoreLib.dll,
       IntuneRemoteManagementPlugin\{Amd64,Arm64,I386}\dynpin_cxx.dll,
       IntuneRemoteManagementPlugin\{Amd64,Arm64,I386}\SignatureValidationLibrary.dll
Operation entry point: Microsoft.Intune.WinRe.Component.WinReComponent (Assembly: Microsoft.Intune.WinRe.Component.dll)
```

Every file in the manifest carries a SHA-256 hash and there's an overall `PackageHash` for the whole CAB — this is a signed/verified bundle, not a loose file drop. The architecture-specific (`Amd64`/`Arm64`/`I386`) layout of `IntuneRemoteManagementPlugin` confirms this bundle is meant to run inside WinRE itself, where only a minimal, non-.NET-hosting environment is available — which is also why `dynpin_cxx.dll` and `SignatureValidationLibrary.dll` (both native) ship per-architecture rather than as a single managed assembly.

### `dynpin_cxx.dll`: a Rust certificate/TLS-pinning validator

This DLL is not .NET — it's a native Rust binary (source path `src\dynpin-cxx\src\dynpinmgr.rs` is embedded in the binary). Its real exported symbols, read directly from the string table:

```text
dynpin_cxx::ffi::DynPinManager::is_payload_signer_trusted
dynpin_cxx::ffi::DynPinManager::is_tls_pinning_satisfied
dynpin_cxx::ffi::DynPinManager::perform_background_update
dynpin_cxx::ffi::DynPinManager::update_periodically
dynpin_cxx::ffi::DynPinManagerConfig::set_config_flags
dynpin_cxx::ffi::create_dyn_pin_manager
dynpin_cxx::ffi::create_dyn_pin_manager_config
dynpin_cxx::ffi::get_ec_point_from_spki
```

This is a "dynamic pinning" manager: it checks whether a downloaded payload's signer is trusted and whether TLS certificate pinning is satisfied, with a background/periodic update mechanism (presumably to refresh the pinned certificate set without requiring an agent upgrade). Given it ships both standalone in `PolicyAdapter\` and inside the WinRE bundle, it's reasonable to read this as the trust-verification gate for content pulled down for both the local Policy Adapter and the WinRE recovery bundle — a sensible design given WinRE has no normal OS security stack to fall back on. This exact file (`dynpin_cxx.dll` in `PolicyAdapter\`) was independently re-extracted in the addendum below and its MD5 hash matched byte-for-byte against the copy analyzed here.

### External confirmation

The filenames, WMI namespace name (`MicrosoftDeviceManagement_IntuneExtensibility`), and WinRE staging pattern match Microsoft's **Quick Machine Recovery (QMR)** feature, part of the **Windows Resiliency Initiative**: a Windows 11 24H2+ capability that lets IT admins push remediation into WinRE when a device fails to boot normally, configured through Intune via the `RemoteRemediation` CSP (`./Device/Vendor/MSFT/RemoteRemediation/CloudRemediationSettings`) or the Settings Catalog. This is the first release in this series where IME visibly ships the client-side agent components for that feature.

### QMR staging and trust-verification flow

The diagram below is reconstructed from the MSI table data, the MOF schema, and the WinRE bundle manifest — the runtime orchestration (left half, dashed) is inferred from the new `IntuneWindowsAgent.exe` types described in section 2 and is not directly observed.

```mermaid
flowchart TD
    A[MSI install] --> B[Stage PolicyAdapter folder:<br/>IntunePolicyAdapter.dll, IntunePolicyStorage.dll,<br/>dynpin_cxx.dll, .mof files]
    A --> C[Stage ClientComponents folder:<br/>LocalCabs, ComponentCache, ComponentConfiguration]
    A --> D[Stage Microsoft.Intune.WinRe.Bundle.cab<br/>+ .json manifest into LocalCabs]

    subgraph Runtime["Agent runtime (inferred, see section 2)"]
        E[ComponentManagerLoader starts<br/>IntunePolicyBackgroundExecutionManager] -.-> F["WMI CIMOM autorecovers<br/>IntunePolicyAdapter.mof on first access<br/>(#pragma autorecover)"]
        F -.-> G[__Namespace MicrosoftDeviceManagement_IntuneExtensibility created]
    end

    D --> H[Intune service delivers a configuration<br/>document: MeID, DocumentID, IntunePayload]
    G --> H
    H --> I[IntunePolicyAdapter.SetTargetResource]
    I --> J[IntunePolicyStorage.dll persists payload<br/>VerifyFileIsMicrosoftSigned checks trust]
    J --> K{Payload references the<br/>WinRE recovery bundle?}
    K -- No --> L[Applied to local WMI providers directly]
    K -- Yes --> M[dynpin_cxx: is_payload_signer_trusted /<br/>is_tls_pinning_satisfied]
    M -- fails --> N[Bundle rejected, not deployed]
    M -- passes --> O[Microsoft.Intune.WinRe.Bundle.cab<br/>staged for WinRE]
    O --> P[On next boot failure, WinRE loads<br/>IntuneRemoteManagementPlugin +<br/>Microsoft.Intune.WinRe.Component.dll]
    P --> Q[Quick Machine Recovery remediation runs<br/>outside the full OS]

    R[Uninstall: REMOVE ~= ALL] --> S[UnregisterIntunePolicyAdapter custom action]
    S --> T["Spawn mofcomp.exe with MofCompTimeoutMilliseconds timeout<br/>against UninstallIntunePolicyAdapter.mof"]
    T --> U[#pragma deleteinstance removes the<br/>MicrosoftDeviceManagement_IntuneExtensibility namespace]
```

## Addendum: confirmed from the actual custom-action assembly

After the report above was written, a second extraction was provided: the raw `IntuneWindowsAgentCustomActions` **Binary table stream** from both the `1.103.101.0` MSI and a `1.104.102.0`-series MSI, each unpacked with 7-Zip. Because that stream is itself a WiX "SfxCA" self-extracting archive, unpacking it exposes the real managed custom-action assembly, `SideCarSetupCustomActions.dll`, rather than just its name in the MSI's `CustomAction` table. This lets several points from section 1 move from *inferred* to *confirmed*, and adds two new findings.

**Version caveat**: the "new" build in this extraction reports `ProductVersion 1.104.56.0000` and `ProductCode {6BC2537A-3B69-40E5-BC1C-0FD9C1CCDAB6}` — a distinct, slightly earlier build than the `1.104.102.0` (`{6A7DFC50-...}`) analyzed in the rest of this report, not the identical package. Its `CustomAction` table is identical to `1.104.102.0`'s, and its copy of `PolicyAdapter\dynpin_cxx.dll` is byte-for-byte identical (MD5 `957473f3501b32bda4e5b3b6cc14b004`) to the one shipped in `1.104.102.0`, so the custom-action findings below should carry over — but this wasn't verified against `1.104.102.0`'s exact custom-action binary, only this adjacent build's.

**Independent cross-check of the MSI table diff.** The extraction included raw `CustomAction.csv`/`Property.csv` table dumps produced by a separate toolchain. They match this report's `msidump`-based findings exactly — same three new actions (`RemoveIntunePoliciesFolder`, `RemoveClientComponentsFolder`, `UnregisterIntunePolicyAdapter`), same unchanged actions, same `ProductVersion`/`ProductCode` pattern.

**The custom-action assembly grew by exactly 6 methods and 1 field** (`TypeDef` unchanged at 29; `MethodDef` 75→81; `Field` 144→145) between the two builds. String diffing accounts for all of it:

```text
New methods:  ConfigureFolderPermissions, GetDefaultInstallationFolderPermissions,
              GetRestrictedPermissions, RemoveClientComponentsFolder,
              RemoveIntunePoliciesFolder, UnregisterIntunePolicyAdapter
New field:    MofCompTimeoutMilliseconds
```

Three of the six confirm the new folder-removal/unregister actions are real, implemented methods (not stubs) exactly matching the MSI table entries. The other three — `ConfigureFolderPermissions`, `GetDefaultInstallationFolderPermissions`, `GetRestrictedPermissions` — are new *private* helpers, which strongly suggests the existing `ConfigureInstallationFolderPermission` custom action (unchanged in the MSI table, sequence renumbered only) was extended in-place to also apply restricted ACLs to the new `PolicyAdapter`/`IntunePolicies`/`ClientComponents` folders, rather than shipping as a separately-named action.

**`MofCompTimeoutMilliseconds` plus new process-launch members** (`get_ExitCode`, `set_WorkingDirectory`, `get_SystemDirectory`, and async stdout/stderr reading via `Task`/`TaskAwaiter`/`GetAwaiter`/`ReadToEndAsync`) appear together in the same diff, and no equivalent set appears anywhere for an install/"Register" path. Read together with the absence of any "RegisterIntunePolicyAdapter" action, this is a reasonably strong (though still not disassembly-level) confirmation of the flow shown in the updated diagram above: **uninstall explicitly launches `mofcomp.exe`** (located via the system directory) **against `UninstallIntunePolicyAdapter.mof`, with a bounded timeout, to tear the WMI namespace down**, while install relies on WMI's own `#pragma autorecover` behavior rather than an explicit compile step.

**File-level cross-check**: the extraction is a genuine administrative install (real per-file NTFS timestamps preserved, e.g. the main agent's PDB path timestamp folder `0716_155833` for the new build vs `0624_211439` for the old one), not a repackaging of this report's own `msiextract` output. Spot-checking `PolicyAdapter\dynpin_cxx.dll` (MD5 match, see above) and the main agent executable's size (435,616 bytes here vs 435,576 bytes in the `1.104.102.0` build analyzed elsewhere in this report — a 40-byte difference consistent with the two adjacent builds rather than an extraction error) gives reasonable confidence the rest of this report's file-size table is accurate.

## 2. A ComponentManager/Policy runtime hosted in the main agent

`Microsoft.Management.Services.IntuneWindowsAgent.exe.config` gains two new trace sources, each writing to its own log file — confirmed directly from the config file diff:

```xml
<source name="OperationsTrace" switchValue="Information">
  <listeners><add name="OperationsTraceFile" ... initializeData="Operations.log" /></listeners>
</source>
<source name="ComponentManager" switchValue="Information">
  <listeners><add name="ComponentManagerTrace" ... initializeData="ComponentManager.log" /></listeners>
</source>
```

`Microsoft.Management.Services.IntuneWindowsAgent.exe` (+26 types, +103 methods over 1.103.101.0) adds matching orchestration types:

```text
IComponentManagerLoader / ComponentRuntimeExecutionManagerFactory / ComponentRuntimeConfiguration
IIntunePolicyBackgroundExecutionManager / IntunePolicyBackgroundExecutionManagerFactory / PolicyRuntimeConfiguration
StartInternalAsync / StopInternalAsync / StopManagerSafeAsync / ShouldOptimizeStartup / StartupOptimizationHelper
ComponentsBaseFolderName / ComponentsBaseRegistryKey / PoliciesBaseFolderName / PolicyAdapterFolderName / PolicyAdapterClass
```

This reads as the main agent process now owning the startup/shutdown lifecycle of the new Components and Policy runtimes — starting them as part of its own service startup sequence (with an optimization path to avoid slowing down normal agent startup) and stopping them safely on shutdown.

### ComponentManager/Policy runtime lifecycle

```mermaid
flowchart TD
    A[IntuneWindowsAgent service starts] --> B{ShouldOptimizeStartup?}
    B -- Yes --> C[Defer non-critical runtime startup<br/>via StartupOptimizationHelper]
    B -- No --> D[Start runtimes inline]
    C --> D
    D --> E[ComponentRuntimeExecutionManagerFactory<br/>creates IComponentManagerLoader]
    D --> F[IntunePolicyBackgroundExecutionManagerFactory<br/>creates IIntunePolicyBackgroundExecutionManager]
    E --> G[StartInternalAsync: Components runtime]
    F --> H[StartInternalAsync: Policy runtime]
    G --> I[Write to ComponentManager.log<br/>via ComponentManager trace source]
    H --> J[Write to Operations.log<br/>via OperationsTrace trace source]
    I --> K[Runtime steady state]
    J --> K
    K --> L[Service stop requested]
    L --> M[StopManagerSafeAsync on each manager]
    M --> N[StopInternalAsync: graceful teardown]
```

## 3. IC3/Trouter telemetry batching

Also new in `IntuneWindowsAgent.exe`:

```text
IC3ConnectionEventStats / IC3ConnectionEventEntry
IC3LoggerErrorStats / IC3LoggerErrorEntry
IC3MessageProcessingStats / IC3MessageProcessingEntry
EmitConnectionEventBatches / EmitLoggerErrorBatches / EmitMessageProcessingBatches / EmitBatched
MaxBufferedConnectionEvents / MaxBufferedLoggerErrors / MaxBufferedMessageProcessing / MaxCharsPerBatch
GetAndResetConnectionEventStats / GetAndResetLoggerErrorStats / GetAndResetMessageProcessingStats
```

This adds a bounded, batched aggregation layer for three categories of IC3/Trouter (the real-time messaging channel) diagnostic events before they're emitted as telemetry, with explicit buffer caps — likely to control telemetry volume/cost rather than emitting one event per connection/logger/message occurrence. `Microsoft.IC3.Trouter.dll` itself also moved `1.2.9.0` → `1.2.18.0` (+4,888 bytes) with new reconnection-resilience members (`CalculateBackoff`, `IReconnectUriStore`, `PersistWithRetryAsync`, a new `AuthenticationError` event).

### Telemetry batching + Trouter reconnect flow

```mermaid
flowchart TD
    A[Trouter connection/logger/message event occurs] --> B{Which category?}
    B -- Connection --> C[IC3ConnectionEventEntry buffered]
    B -- Logger error --> D[IC3LoggerErrorEntry buffered]
    B -- Message processing --> E[IC3MessageProcessingEntry buffered]
    C --> F{Buffer >= MaxBufferedConnectionEvents<br/>or MaxCharsPerBatch reached?}
    D --> G{Buffer >= MaxBufferedLoggerErrors?}
    E --> H{Buffer >= MaxBufferedMessageProcessing?}
    F -- Yes --> I[EmitConnectionEventBatches]
    G -- Yes --> J[EmitLoggerErrorBatches]
    H -- Yes --> K[EmitMessageProcessingBatches]
    F -- No --> L[Keep buffering]
    G -- No --> L
    H -- No --> L
    I --> M[GetAndResetConnectionEventStats]
    J --> N[GetAndResetLoggerErrorStats]
    K --> O[GetAndResetMessageProcessingStats]

    P[Trouter socket error] --> Q[onSocketNetworkErrorAsync]
    Q --> R[CalculateBackoff]
    R --> S[IReconnectUriStore.LoadAsync cached URI]
    S --> T[Reconnect attempt]
    T -- auth fails --> U[raise AuthenticationError event /<br/>HandleAuthenticationError]
    T -- succeeds --> V[PersistWithRetryAsync stores new URI]
```

## 4. EnterpriseDesktopAppManagement MSI key cleanup

New in both `IntuneWindowsAgent.exe` and `ScriptPlugIn.dll`:

```text
DeleteEnterpriseDesktopAppManagementMsiKey
EnterpriseDesktopAppManagementCleanupSnapshot
EnterpriseDesktopAppManagementCleanupTelemetryContext
```

This looks like a targeted cleanup routine for stale registry keys left behind under the `EnterpriseDesktopAppManagement` CSP node (the CSP Win32 app installs register under), with its own before/after telemetry snapshot — consistent with a fix for orphaned MSI registration data rather than a new feature.

### Cleanup flow

```mermaid
flowchart TD
    A[Agent startup or post-upgrade pass] --> B[Scan EnterpriseDesktopAppManagement<br/>CSP registry node for MSI keys]
    B --> C[EnterpriseDesktopAppManagementCleanupSnapshot:<br/>record before-state]
    C --> D{Stale/orphaned MSI key found?}
    D -- No --> E[No action, snapshot discarded]
    D -- Yes --> F[DeleteEnterpriseDesktopAppManagementMsiKey]
    F --> G[Record after-state]
    G --> H[Emit EnterpriseDesktopAppManagementCleanupTelemetryContext<br/>before vs after diff]
```

## 5. Win32 app enforcement: OperationSpan tracing and download telemetry

`Win32AppPlugIn.dll` grew the most of any managed plug-in this release (+23,040 bytes, +26 types, +111 methods). New members:

```text
OperationSpan / TrackRoot / TrackRootAsync / ProcessSubgraph
Win32AppOperationSpanTelemetryEnabledKey / Win32AppEnforcementTelemetryEnabledKey
EmitEnforcementCompleted / GetEnforcementErrorCode
DownloadStartTimeKey / DownloadCompletionTimeKey / DownloadJobCompletionTimeKey
DownloadCheckInCountKey / DownloadStatusKey / SetDownloadInitiated / SetDownloadStatus
EnsurePowerEventListenerStarted
```

This is a tree/span-based operation-tracing model (root → subgraph) for Win32 app enforcement, flight-gated by two separate telemetry-enabled keys, plus explicit timestamps for download start/completion/job-completion and a check-in counter — more granular visibility into why and how long an app install took, and a power-event listener addition (possibly to account for sleep/hibernate interruptions during long downloads).

### OperationSpan + download telemetry flow

```mermaid
flowchart TD
    A[Win32 app enforcement cycle starts] --> B{Win32AppOperationSpanTelemetryEnabledKey?}
    B -- No --> C[Legacy enforcement path, no span tracking]
    B -- Yes --> D[TrackRootAsync: open root OperationSpan]
    D --> E[ProcessSubgraph: detection]
    D --> F[ProcessSubgraph: download]
    D --> G[ProcessSubgraph: install/enforcement]
    F --> H[SetDownloadInitiated / DownloadStartTimeKey]
    H --> I[EnsurePowerEventListenerStarted<br/>watch for sleep/hibernate interruption]
    I --> J[Download progresses,<br/>DownloadCheckInCountKey increments]
    J --> K{Download finished?}
    K -- Yes --> L[DownloadCompletionTimeKey +<br/>DownloadJobCompletionTimeKey recorded]
    K -- No / retried --> J
    G --> M{Enforcement succeeded?}
    M -- Yes --> N[MarkSuccess]
    M -- No --> O[MarkFailed + GetEnforcementErrorCode]
    L --> P[Close subgraph span]
    N --> P
    O --> P
    P --> Q{Win32AppEnforcementTelemetryEnabledKey?}
    Q -- Yes --> R[EmitEnforcementCompleted<br/>with full span + download timings]
    Q -- No --> S[Span discarded, no emission]
```

## 6. Script plug-in: policy security mode

New in `ScriptPlugIn.dll`:

```text
PolicySecurityMode / LogDecryptionFailure / LogDecryptionSuccess
```

Combined with the new Policy Adapter infrastructure, this suggests script content delivered through the new policy channel may be encrypted, with the plug-in now logging decrypt success/failure explicitly. This is inferred from naming; no decryption algorithm or key-handling detail is visible from strings alone.

## 7. DataSensor plug-in: scale-unit reuse, EPM telemetry suppression, WinRE tie-in

New in `DataSensorPlugIn.dll`:

```text
GetCurrentScaleUnit / ScaleUnitHelper / BuildPropsWithScaleUnit / BLEU_URL
ECSFlightKeyEnableComponentManager / ECSFlightKeyDisableInstanceLevelEpmEvents
SuppressedInstanceLevelEpmTelemetryLock / EmitSuppressedInstanceLevelEpmTelemetryIfNeeded
WinReAgent
```

`ScaleUnitHelper`/`GetCurrentScaleUnit` reuse the `EnvironmentResolver` scale-unit logic (present unchanged since 1.102) at the data-sensor level. `ECSFlightKeyEnableComponentManager` and the literal string `WinReAgent` tie this plug-in's telemetry directly to the new ComponentManager/WinRE feature area. Separately, a new suppression mechanism for "instance-level EPM" (Endpoint Privilege Management) telemetry events reduces event volume when a flight disables it, tracked with an accumulated-suppressed-count and a time window.

## 8. Administrative PowerShell module: `PolicyStorageCmdlets.dll`

A managed cmdlet module (`CmdletAttribute`, `ProcessRecord`, `OutputTypeAttribute` present) that reads the local Policy Storage registry hive directly (`OpenHive`, `GetInt32Value`, `GetStringValue`, `ParsePolicyKeyName`, `TargetClassName`). No literal `Verb-Noun` cmdlet name strings were found in the string table (likely defined via attribute parameters resolved only at reflection time), so exact cmdlet names can't be confirmed from static strings alone — but this is clearly a troubleshooting/inspection module for whatever `IntunePolicyStorage.dll` persists locally.

## 9. No functional change, despite file churn

Metadata row counts (TypeDef/MethodDef/Field/Property) are **identical** between 1.103.101.0 and 1.104.102.0 for:

- `Microsoft.Management.Clients.Flighting.dll` (46/204/150/39 both versions) — version stamp moved `6.1.365.171` → `7.1.1.28` and size shrank 232 bytes, but no structural change.
- `Microsoft.Management.Clients.IntuneManagementExtension.TamperProtection.dll` (33/143/70/7 both versions) — file grew +1,024 bytes but the only new strings are build/PDB/signature timestamp metadata, not new certificate names or types.
- `ClientHealthEval.exe` — counts essentially flat (one fewer method, one fewer field); not a functional change.

`Microsoft.Management.Clients.Common.Base.dll`/`Common.Telemetry.dll`/`Common.EtwProvider.Sidecar.dll` all moved from the `7.1.1.18` to the `7.1.1.32` version stamp with only single-digit-KB size deltas — consistent with the whole `7.1.1.x`-versioned component family (which now also includes the six new Policy/Components DLLs) being rebuilt together, not a targeted functional change to those three specific files.

`WindowsPackageManager.dll` and `Microsoft.Management.Deployment.InProc.dll` are **byte-identical in size** to 1.103.101.0 (0 delta) — no WinGet engine change this release, unlike the 1.102→1.103 step.

## 10. .NET dependency refresh

The `System.*`/`Microsoft.Extensions.*`/`Microsoft.Bcl.*` family that was bumped to file version `10.0.626.17701` in the 1.102→1.103 step moves again, to `10.0.926.27113`, and the `.exe.config` binding redirects move in lockstep from `0.0.0.0-10.0.0.6` to `0.0.0.0-10.0.0.9` across every affected assembly reference — a routine monthly-cadence BCL package refresh, not a functional agent change.

## 11. MSI mechanics

New components install to `PolicyAdapter`, `IntunePolicies` (currently empty of files — likely populated at runtime once policies are received), and three `ClientComponents` subfolders. Three new uninstall-time custom actions clean up after themselves:

```text
RemoveIntunePoliciesFolder     REMOVE ~= "ALL" AND (NOT UPGRADINGPRODUCTCODE)   seq 6592
RemoveClientComponentsFolder   REMOVE ~= "ALL" AND (NOT UPGRADINGPRODUCTCODE)   seq 6591
UnregisterIntunePolicyAdapter  REMOVE ~= "ALL" AND (NOT UPGRADINGPRODUCTCODE)   seq 3499
```

All other `InstallExecuteSequence` renumbering in the diff (`CreateRegistryKeyAndValues`, `RegisterAUMID`, etc. shifting by 1–2 sequence numbers) is purely because the new actions were inserted into the table — the conditions and relative order of existing actions are unchanged. `Property`, `Upgrade`, and `Signature` reflect only the expected version bump. This table-level diff was independently cross-checked against the raw `CustomAction.csv`/`Property.csv` dumps in the addendum and matched exactly.

## Evidence and confidence

### High confidence (file content directly readable, new types/strings absent from 1.103.101.0, or independently cross-checked)

- Quick Machine Recovery client plumbing: MOF-registered `IntunePolicyAdapter` WMI/DSC provider, the WinRE bundle manifest and its listed components, and `dynpin_cxx.dll`'s Rust-native pinning/signature-trust functions — all read directly from shipped text files or exported symbol names, not inferred.
- `UnregisterIntunePolicyAdapter` invoking `mofcomp.exe` (with a timeout) against `UninstallIntunePolicyAdapter.mof` at uninstall — confirmed from new process-launch members and a new `MofCompTimeoutMilliseconds` field in the actual custom-action assembly (addendum).
- The three new folder-removal/unregister custom actions are real implemented methods, and `ConfigureInstallationFolderPermission` was extended with new private helpers to secure the new folders — confirmed from the custom-action assembly's method-level diff (addendum).
- New ComponentManager/Policy runtime lifecycle management in the main agent, confirmed by both new types and new `.exe.config` trace sources/log files.
- IC3/Trouter batched telemetry layer with explicit buffer caps.
- Win32 app enforcement `OperationSpan` tracing and download timing telemetry.
- `Flighting.dll`, `TamperProtection.dll`, and `ClientHealthEval.exe` are functionally unchanged this release (identical or near-identical metadata row counts).
- `WindowsPackageManager.dll`/WinGet engine unchanged this release.
- This report's MSI table diff and file inventory were independently cross-checked against a second extraction and matched (see addendum), aside from the noted `1.104.56.0000` vs `1.104.102.0` build discrepancy.

### Inferred, not confirmed

- The exact purpose of the SHA-256-shaped hex strings following `VerifyFileIsMicrosoftSigned` in `IntunePolicyStorage.dll` (read as a pinned hash allow-list, not independently verified).
- That `ScriptPlugIn.dll`'s new `PolicySecurityMode`/decryption logging is connected to policy content delivered via the new Policy Adapter (plausible given the shared timing and naming, not directly traced).
- That the custom-action findings in the addendum (built against `1.104.56.0000`) apply unchanged to the exact `1.104.102.0` build — supported by an identical `CustomAction` table and a byte-identical `dynpin_cxx.dll`, but not verified against `1.104.102.0`'s own custom-action binary directly.

### Flight or server dependent

- `ECSFlightKeyEnableComponentManager` / `ECSFlightKeyDisableInstanceLevelEpmEvents`
- `Win32AppOperationSpanTelemetryEnabledKey` / `Win32AppEnforcementTelemetryEnabledKey`
- Whatever server-side flight ultimately controls QMR/`RemoteRemediation` CSP enablement itself (outside IME's own binaries)

## Suggested validation

**Quick Machine Recovery**: on a test device with Windows 11 24H2+, enable Quick Machine Recovery / Cloud Remediation via the `RemoteRemediation` CSP or Settings Catalog, then check for the `MicrosoftDeviceManagement_IntuneExtensibility` WMI namespace (`Get-WmiObject -Namespace root -List` should show it once registered) and confirm the `PolicyAdapter`/`ClientComponents` folders populate under the IME install directory.

**ComponentManager runtime**: after upgrading to `1.104.102.0`, check for `Operations.log` and `ComponentManager.log` alongside IME's other log files, and confirm they contain startup/shutdown entries on service restart.

**Win32 app telemetry**: install a Win32 app and confirm the new download-timing keys (start/completion/job-completion/check-in count) appear in the resulting telemetry payload when the relevant flight is enabled.

**EnterpriseDesktopAppManagement cleanup**: on a device with legacy/orphaned MSI keys under the EnterpriseDesktopAppManagement CSP node, confirm they're removed and a cleanup telemetry event is emitted after upgrade.

**Uninstall MOF cleanup**: uninstall IME from a `1.104.102.0` device and confirm (via Process Monitor or an ETW trace) that `mofcomp.exe` is actually launched, and that the `MicrosoftDeviceManagement_IntuneExtensibility` WMI namespace is gone afterward.

## Final assessment

`1.104.102.0` is a significantly larger release than any prior step in this series, and for the first time the change is externally attributable to a named, documented Microsoft feature: Quick Machine Recovery under the Windows Resiliency Initiative. IME is no longer just a policy/app/script delivery agent — this release adds a general-purpose local policy adapter (WMI/DSC-based), a component-cache/staging subsystem, and the specific WinRE recovery bundle QMR depends on, secured by a dedicated native certificate-pinning component. Everything else in the release — Win32 app tracing, IC3 telemetry batching, the .NET dependency refresh, and the EnterpriseDesktopAppManagement cleanup — is comparatively incremental. Several files that grew or moved version numbers (`Flighting.dll`, `TamperProtection.dll`, `Common.Base/Telemetry/EtwProvider.Sidecar.dll`) show no structural change and appear to be along for the ride in a shared build/versioning wave rather than independently modified. A second, independently-produced extraction of the same packages (see the addendum after section 1) cross-checked the MSI table diff and file inventory, and gave direct access to the custom-action assembly itself — upgrading the registration/uninstall mechanics from an educated guess to a confirmed finding.

